//! Append-only history of everything that changed the world, and the rollback
//! built on top of it.
//!
//! # The model
//!
//! The world on disk is **not** a copy of the world in memory. It is:
//!
//! ```text
//! world = generator(seed)          the baseline, recomputed on demand
//!       + journal                  every change anyone ever made
//! ```
//!
//! The generator is deterministic, so an untouched column costs nothing to
//! store — it is regenerated byte-for-byte whenever it is needed. Only the
//! changes are written down. A world where a hundred players walked around and
//! placed a hundred blocks stores a hundred entries, not a hundred megabytes of
//! terrain.
//!
//! Each [`Event`] carries both sides of its change, so the log runs in both
//! directions: forwards to rebuild the current state, backwards to undo. That
//! is the whole of the rollback.
//!
//! # Keys
//!
//! Three key spaces share one backend, distinguished by a leading byte, and all
//! three end in a big-endian sequence number so byte order *is* chronological
//! order:
//!
//! ```text
//! 'E' seq(8)                       -> the encoded event
//! 'X' cx(4) cz(4) seq(8)           -> empty; "column cx,cz was touched at seq"
//! 'P' actor(16) seq(8)             -> empty; "this actor acted at seq"
//! 'H'                              -> the high-water sequence number
//! ```
//!
//! The two index spaces hold no payload: they exist so that "what happened in
//! this column" and "what did this player do" are prefix scans rather than a
//! walk of the entire history. Storing the payload once and pointing at it
//! twice also means an event can never disagree with itself.
//!
//! # What a rollback is
//!
//! [`Journal::plan_rollback`] selects events by actor, by area and by time, and
//! returns the block changes that undo them **newest first**. Applying them in
//! that order is what makes overlapping edits come out right: if a player put
//! stone over dirt and then diamond over the stone, undoing newest-first
//! restores stone and then dirt. Undoing oldest-first would leave the diamond.
//!
//! The undo is itself recorded as new events, attributed to whoever ordered it.
//! History is never rewritten — that is the difference between a rollback and
//! a deletion, and it is why a rollback can itself be rolled back.

